Z||000000||DXR_DAD||04-20-99||09:13 PM||||Cruise control not working, I looked in the engine compartment and found some vac. hoses missing and I can not find a diagram in my Haynes. Can anyone help???
Z||000001||theogre||04-20-99||09:30 PM||||most of the vac diagram is on the VECI lable under the deck lid. Which motor, and what year? I know the 4 cyls only use about 5 vac lines on the cars w/o cruise. 2 to the vapor can, 1 to the MAP, 1 to the brake booster, and one to the EGR. Those should all be on the VECI lable.<p>Cruise units just need a source of manifold vacuum. doesn't matter where on the manifold much. look for an outlet thats about the same size as the one on the cruise servo. It's usually bigger than the emisions vac lines. It could be a "T" off the brake booster. (I'm not sure where the fitting would be. this is based on all the other cruise units I've delt with. haven't been able to find a Fiero one yet.)<p>
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||04-20-99||10:54 PM||||86 SE V6, I will look for the lable. I think the line is missing on the servo??<p>Thanks orge
Z||000003||theogre||04-20-99||11:39 PM||||U.S. law at least requires the VECI (Vehicle Emision Control Information) label to be in the engine compartment of all cars after, i think, the 1973 model year. On Fiero, it is usually on the deck lid. On some Fiero there is another lable that tells you how the belts go. (at least there is on my 87)<p>The label tells you, depending on the car, Plug gap, emision/vac line routing, Idle RPM, ingition timing(curb). The vac line routing doesn't have to include all the lines, just the emision related ones.
Z||000004||theogre||04-20-99||11:40 PM||||P.S. if the line is missing off the servo,,,,, you may have other problems. It may have been removed because the servo was toast.
